===== STATUS: success =====
Question: How do large language models perform structured multi-step reasoning?

Large language models perform structured multi-step reasoning through chain-of-thought prompting, a technique that prompts models to explicitly generate intermediate reasoning steps before arriving at final answers (wei2022chainofthoughtpromptingelicits pages 7-9).

The methodology involves manually composing chains of thought in few-shot exemplars, which models then follow when solving new problems (wei2022chainofthoughtpromptingelicits pages 7-9). This approach requires no model fine-tuning and works with off-the-shelf language models such as PaLM, LaMDA, and GPT-3 (wei2022chainofthoughtpromptingelicits pages 7-9).

Chain-of-thought prompting demonstrates substantial performance improvements across three reasoning domains: arithmetic reasoning, commonsense reasoning, and symbolic reasoning (wei2022chainofthoughtpromptingelicits pages 7-9). The technique produces dramatically increasing scaling curves compared to standard prompting, with reasoning abilities emerging primarily at larger model scales around 100 billion parameters (wei2022chainofthoughtpromptingelicits pages 7-9).

A notable capability of this approach is its facilitation of out-of-domain generalization, enabling models to handle longer sequences than those encountered in training examples (wei2022chainofthoughtpromptingelicits pages 7-9). These findings indicate that explicit intermediate reasoning steps are a critical mechanism for eliciting complex reasoning in large language models.

References

1. (wei2022chainofthoughtpromptingelicits pages 7-9): Jason Wei, Xuezhi Wang, Dale Schuurmans, Maarten Bosma, Brian Ichter, Fei Xia, Ed Chi, Quoc V Le, and Denny Zhou. Chain-of-thought prompting elicits reasoning in large language models. neural information processing systems, Jan 2022. URL: https://doi.org/10.52202/068431-1800, doi:10.52202/068431-1800. This article has 14100 citations and is from a highest quality peer-reviewed journal.
